From: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: add a host-bridge link-recovery hook and use it to revive
 a wedged MHI endpoint

An endpoint that drops the PCIe link cannot be recovered from the endpoint side,
and on some hosts it must not even be touched while the link is down: on
Qualcomm IP rev 2.9.0 a CPU access to a downed link never completes, so it hangs
rather than failing (see the preceding patch for why the completion timeout is
off). On an IPQ5018 CPE with a 5G modem on pcie1 and a 1.5 s external watchdog,
that hang is a silent reboot.

What can recover such a link is PERST# plus a controller re-init, which only the
host driver can do. Re-probing it is not available: qcom-pcie has no ->remove and
sets suppress_bind_attrs, so device_release_driver() + device_attach() leaves the
controller unbound for good, which is strictly worse than doing nothing. A
device-specific endpoint reset does not help either, because re-initialising the
endpoint still has to cross a link that is not up yet.

The sequence is already in the host driver, just unreachable at runtime:
qcom_pcie_suspend_noirq() calls ->deinit when the link is down and
qcom_pcie_resume_noirq() calls ->init to bring it back, both on an
already-probed controller, and dw_pcie_resume_noirq() shows the full
init/setup_rc/start_link shape. Add a ->link_recover hook to struct
pci_host_bridge so a host driver can offer it, implement it for qcom with an
elapsed-time link-up deadline long enough for an endpoint that reboots its own
firmware (dw_pcie_wait_for_link()'s bound is well under a second; this modem has
been observed to need 20-30 s), and call it from mhi-pci-generic.

The hook's contract is what makes it usable: 0 means the link is up and the
controller is powered, so the caller may address the endpoint again - a statement
about the host at that instant, not a promise that the endpoint is ready, that it
is the same endpoint, or that the link will still be up for the next access. The
qcom implementation touches only SoC-local resources: clocks, PHY, the PERST#
GPIO, DBI and the local link-status register. That audit is specific to
cfg_2_9_0, since cfg_2_7_0's qcom_pcie_host_post_init_2_7_0() walks the PCI bus,
so the implementation refuses every other variant. Hardware-off state is tracked
separately from ->suspended because a failed recovery leaves clocks and PHY down
and a retry must not release them twice.

On the MHI side this is one operation rather than separate quiesce/relink/restore
triggers, so no caller can run the phases out of order, and the recovery mutex is
held across all of it - the runtime-PM manipulation and the teardown included.
Publishing a "recovery in progress" flag before taking the lock would let a
second writer see the flag, skip the teardown as though it had already happened,
and reset the host underneath a still-running MHI stack.

Runtime PM is excluded with __pm_runtime_disable() rather than a usage reference
or a flag tested inside the driver's PM callbacks: a usage reference only
prevents idle suspension, and checking inside the callbacks excludes the wrong
layer, because pci_pm_runtime_resume() restores config space via
pci_pm_default_resume_early() before the driver callback is reached at all. It is
called with check_resume = false rather than through the pm_runtime_disable()
wrapper, which passes true and so deliberately carries out a pending resume
first - on a PCI device that means restoring endpoint configuration across the
very link this exists because it cannot be touched. The barrier that waits out an
already-running transition is kept either way, and MHI_PCI_DEV_SUSPENDED is
re-checked after it, since a suspend in flight when this started may have
completed meanwhile.

Quiescing the device needs more than powering MHI down: MHI_PCI_DEV_QUIESCED
stops health_check() queueing recovery and makes mhi_pci_recovery_work() return
early, and the worker is cancelled, then the timer deleted, then the worker
cancelled again, because the timer can queue it in between and that worker would
otherwise run a stale recovery after the device is back.
mhi_power_down(graceful=false) then forces MHI_PM_LD_ERR_FATAL_DETECT, which
MHI_REG_ACCESS_VALID() excludes, so the MHI core stops accessing registers.

Because the hook cycles the whole link, the caller first checks that this
endpoint really is the only device under the host - off its single root port,
with no siblings and nothing below it - and refuses otherwise. Before restoring
the saved configuration the endpoint's identity is checked, since a device that
came back in a different mode would otherwise be given another device's BARs. A
device that is already runtime-suspended is rejected rather than handled, as
reconciling that would mean resuming the endpoint over the link this exists to
avoid using. On failure the device stays quiesced with runtime PM disabled, so
the write can simply be retried.

A single write-only attribute drives it ("recover", or "recover <timeout_ms>"),
so a userspace modem supervisor can use it as a recovery step before falling back
to rebooting the board.

Signed-off-by: Muhammad Huda Nugroho <mhudan21@gmail.com>
---
--- a/include/linux/pci.h
+++ b/include/linux/pci.h
@@ -637,6 +637,29 @@
 			resource_size_t start,
 			resource_size_t size,
 			resource_size_t align);
+
+	/*
+	 * Optional host-controller-driven link recovery: cycle the link
+	 * (typically PERST# plus a controller re-init) using only resources
+	 * local to the host, touching nothing downstream.
+	 *
+	 * @timeout_ms bounds the wait for the link to come back; 0 means the
+	 * implementation's default, and an implementation may clamp a value it
+	 * considers unreasonable rather than fail - a caller has usually
+	 * stopped using the endpoint before calling, so failing late on an
+	 * argument would leave it down for no reason.
+	 *
+	 * Returns 0 once the link is up again and the controller is powered.
+	 * That is a statement about the host at that instant: it does not mean
+	 * the endpoint is ready, that it is the same endpoint, or that the link
+	 * will still be up for the caller's next access.
+	 *
+	 * The whole link is cycled, so every device below the host is reset.
+	 * The caller is responsible for having quiesced them. May sleep.
+	 */
+	int (*link_recover)(struct pci_host_bridge *bridge,
+			    unsigned int timeout_ms);
+
 	unsigned long	private[] ____cacheline_aligned;
 };
 
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
 #include <linux/limits.h>
 #include <linux/init.h>
+#include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/of_pci.h>
 #include <linux/pci.h>
@@ -284,6 +285,13 @@
 	struct dentry *debugfs;
 	struct list_head ports;
 	bool suspended;
+	/*
+	 * Set while the controller's clocks and PHY are off because a link
+	 * recovery failed. Distinct from @suspended: it says the HARDWARE is
+	 * down, not that the system is asleep, and it is what keeps a retry
+	 * from releasing resources it has already released.
+	 */
+	bool relink_hw_off;
 	bool use_pm_opp;
 };
 
@@ -1802,6 +1810,164 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Host-side link recovery.
+ *
+ * When an endpoint drops the PCIe link - an SDX6x modem does this on every
+ * subsystem restart - the only thing that can bring it back on this hardware is
+ * PERST# plus a controller re-init, and the endpoint cannot be touched to get
+ * there: a CPU access to a downed link never completes here, so an access that
+ * would merely fail elsewhere hangs the CPU. Re-probing the controller is not an
+ * option either - qcom-pcie has no ->remove and sets suppress_bind_attrs, so
+ * device_release_driver() leaves it unbound for good.
+ *
+ * Everything here is local SoC MMIO plus the PERST# GPIO: ->deinit/->init are
+ * the same callbacks qcom_pcie_suspend_noirq()/qcom_pcie_resume_noirq() already
+ * run on this very controller once the link is down, dw_pcie_setup_rc() writes
+ * only DBI, and dw_pcie_link_up() reads a local status register. That audit was
+ * done for the IPQ5018 configuration; it does not hold for every variant
+ * (cfg_2_7_0's qcom_pcie_host_post_init_2_7_0() walks the PCI bus to set ASPM),
+ * so this refuses to run anywhere else.
+ */
+#define QCOM_PCIE_RELINK_PERST_MS	100
+#define QCOM_PCIE_RELINK_POLL_MS	100
+#define QCOM_PCIE_RELINK_TIMEOUT_MS	30000
+#define QCOM_PCIE_RELINK_TIMEOUT_MAX	60000
+
+/* One controller at a time; serialises recovery against itself. */
+static DEFINE_MUTEX(qcom_pcie_relink_mutex);
+
+static int qcom_pcie_relink(struct qcom_pcie *pcie, unsigned int timeout_ms)
+{
+	struct dw_pcie *pci = pcie->pci;
+	struct dw_pcie_rp *pp = &pci->pp;
+	unsigned long deadline, started;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (pcie->suspended)
+		return -EBUSY;
+
+	/*
+	 * Tear down only what is actually up: ->deinit over already-released
+	 * resources would double-decrement the PHY and the clocks (see the
+	 * unwind comment in qcom_pcie_phy_power_on()).
+	 */
+	if (!pcie->relink_hw_off) {
+		/*
+		 * No-op on this driver - qcom's dw_pcie_ops has no .stop_link -
+		 * but correct for any variant that grows one. PERST# below is
+		 * what actually brings the link down.
+		 */
+		dw_pcie_stop_link(pci);
+		if (pp->ops->deinit)
+			pp->ops->deinit(pp);
+		pcie->relink_hw_off = true;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * ->deinit asserted PERST#, and qcom_ep_reset_deassert() adds another
+	 * PCIE_T_PVPERL_MS before releasing it, so the total assertion is at
+	 * least twice this. The wait here is deliberate extra margin for an
+	 * endpoint that needs convincing to restart, not the T_PVPERL minimum.
+	 */
+	msleep(QCOM_PCIE_RELINK_PERST_MS);
+
+	if (pp->ops->init) {
+		ret = pp->ops->init(pp);
+		if (ret) {
+			/*
+			 * qcom_pcie_host_init() unwinds its own partial state,
+			 * so the hardware is off and must not be deinitialised
+			 * a second time.
+			 */
+			dev_err(pci->dev, "relink: host init failed: %d\n", ret);
+			return ret;
+		}
+	}
+
+	pcie->relink_hw_off = false;
+
+	/*
+	 * The GCC block reset in ->init wiped the DWC core registers, so the
+	 * root complex configuration and the outbound iATU windows have to be
+	 * reprogrammed before any config access is attempted.
+	 */
+	ret = dw_pcie_setup_rc(pp);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(pci->dev, "relink: setup_rc failed: %d\n", ret);
+		goto err_deinit;
+	}
+
+	ret = dw_pcie_start_link(pci);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(pci->dev, "relink: start_link failed: %d\n", ret);
+		goto err_deinit;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Poll locally instead of dw_pcie_wait_for_link(): its bound is under a
+	 * second, which is fine for a cold boot but not for an endpoint that
+	 * reboots its own firmware first. Measure elapsed time rather than
+	 * counting sleeps, which only ever under-counts.
+	 */
+	started = jiffies;
+	deadline = started + msecs_to_jiffies(timeout_ms);
+	while (!dw_pcie_link_up(pci)) {
+		if (time_after(jiffies, deadline)) {
+			dev_err(pci->dev, "relink: no link after %u ms\n",
+				timeout_ms);
+			ret = -ETIMEDOUT;
+			goto err_stop_link;
+		}
+		msleep(QCOM_PCIE_RELINK_POLL_MS);
+	}
+
+	if (pp->ops->post_init)
+		pp->ops->post_init(pp);
+
+	dev_info(pci->dev, "relink: link up after %u ms\n",
+		 jiffies_to_msecs(jiffies - started));
+
+	return 0;
+
+err_stop_link:
+	dw_pcie_stop_link(pci);
+err_deinit:
+	if (pp->ops->deinit)
+		pp->ops->deinit(pp);
+	pcie->relink_hw_off = true;
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static int qcom_pcie_link_recover(struct pci_host_bridge *bridge,
+				  unsigned int timeout_ms)
+{
+	struct qcom_pcie *pcie = dev_get_drvdata(bridge->dev.parent);
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!pcie)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	if (pcie->cfg != &cfg_2_9_0_ipq5018)
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+
+	/*
+	 * Clamp rather than reject: the caller has already stopped using the
+	 * endpoint by the time it gets here, so failing on an argument would
+	 * leave the device down for no reason at all.
+	 */
+	if (!timeout_ms)
+		timeout_ms = QCOM_PCIE_RELINK_TIMEOUT_MS;
+	timeout_ms = min(timeout_ms, (unsigned int)QCOM_PCIE_RELINK_TIMEOUT_MAX);
+
+	mutex_lock(&qcom_pcie_relink_mutex);
+	ret = qcom_pcie_relink(pcie, timeout_ms);
+	mutex_unlock(&qcom_pcie_relink_mutex);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
 static int qcom_pcie_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	const struct qcom_pcie_cfg *pcie_cfg;
@@ -1970,6 +2136,14 @@
 		goto err_phy_exit;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Offer host-driven link recovery to whatever ends up bound downstream.
+	 * Installed here because dw_pcie_host_init() is what allocates
+	 * pp->bridge; unlike the native_aer/native_pme suppression in
+	 * qcom_pcie_host_init(), nothing reads this during pci_host_probe().
+	 */
+	pp->bridge->link_recover = qcom_pcie_link_recover;
+
 	qcom_pcie_icc_opp_update(pcie);
 
 	if (pcie->mhi)
--- a/drivers/bus/mhi/host/pci_generic.c
+++ b/drivers/bus/mhi/host/pci_generic.c
@@ -13,7 +13,9 @@
 #include <linux/mhi.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/pci.h>
+#include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
+#include <linux/string.h>
 #include <linux/timer.h>
 #include <linux/workqueue.h>
 
@@ -1024,6 +1026,13 @@
 enum mhi_pci_device_status {
 	MHI_PCI_DEV_STARTED,
 	MHI_PCI_DEV_SUSPENDED,
+	/*
+	 * Host-driven link recovery in progress: the link is presumed dead and
+	 * nothing may touch the device until it is cleared. Runtime PM is
+	 * disabled for as long as it is set. Only ever changed under
+	 * recovery_mutex.
+	 */
+	MHI_PCI_DEV_QUIESCED,
 };
 
 struct mhi_pci_device {
@@ -1031,6 +1040,8 @@
 	struct pci_saved_state *pci_state;
 	struct work_struct recovery_work;
 	struct timer_list health_check_timer;
+	/* Serialises the whole of link_recovery_store() against itself. */
+	struct mutex recovery_mutex;
 	unsigned long status;
 	bool reset_on_remove;
 };
@@ -1198,6 +1209,9 @@
 	struct pci_dev *pdev = to_pci_dev(mhi_cntrl->cntrl_dev);
 	int err;
 
+	if (test_bit(MHI_PCI_DEV_QUIESCED, &mhi_pdev->status))
+		return;
+
 	dev_warn(&pdev->dev, "device recovery started\n");
 
 	if (!pdev->is_virtfn)
@@ -1243,6 +1257,249 @@
 		dev_err(&pdev->dev, "Recovery failed: %d\n", err);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The host-driven link recovery below cycles the whole link, so every device
+ * under the bridge is reset whether or not its driver was told. Refuse unless
+ * this endpoint is demonstrably the only thing down there: it hangs directly
+ * off the host's single root port, has no siblings, and nothing is enumerated
+ * below it. The topology is static on the hardware this was written for; this
+ * is a guard against being used somewhere it does not belong, not a claim to
+ * be race-free against concurrent enumeration or removal.
+ */
+static bool mhi_pci_sole_device_on_host(struct pci_dev *pdev,
+					struct pci_host_bridge *bridge)
+{
+	struct pci_dev *rp = pdev->bus->self;
+	struct pci_dev *dev;
+	unsigned int n;
+
+	if (!rp || rp->bus != bridge->bus)
+		return false;
+
+	if (!list_empty(&pdev->bus->children))
+		return false;
+
+	n = 0;
+	list_for_each_entry(dev, &pdev->bus->devices, bus_list)
+		n++;
+	if (n != 1)
+		return false;
+
+	n = 0;
+	list_for_each_entry(dev, &bridge->bus->devices, bus_list)
+		n++;
+
+	return n == 1;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Recovery for an endpoint that has dropped the PCIe link.
+ *
+ * mhi_pci_recovery_work() cannot handle that case: it goes from
+ * mhi_power_down() straight to pci_restore_state() and mhi_pci_is_alive(),
+ * which only works while the endpoint is still reachable. Once the link is
+ * gone, only the host controller can bring it back, and on some SoCs the
+ * endpoint must not be touched meanwhile - on IPQ5018 a CPU access to a downed
+ * link never completes, so a stray register read is not an error but a hang,
+ * and on a board with a short external watchdog that hang is a silent reboot.
+ *
+ * This is one operation rather than separate quiesce/relink/restore triggers,
+ * so no caller can run the phases out of order, and recovery_mutex is held
+ * across ALL of it - including the runtime-PM manipulation and the teardown.
+ * Publishing a "someone is recovering" flag before taking the lock would let a
+ * second writer see the flag, skip the teardown as though it had already
+ * happened, and reset the host underneath a still-running MHI stack.
+ *
+ * Runtime PM is excluded with __pm_runtime_disable() rather than a usage
+ * reference or a flag tested inside the driver's callbacks. A usage reference
+ * only prevents idle suspension, and checking inside the callbacks excludes the
+ * wrong layer: pci_pm_runtime_resume() restores config space via
+ * pci_pm_default_resume_early() before the driver callback is reached at all.
+ *
+ * It is called with check_resume = false, NOT via the pm_runtime_disable()
+ * wrapper: that wrapper passes true, which deliberately carries out a pending
+ * resume request first, and on a PCI device that means restoring endpoint
+ * configuration across the very link this operation exists because it cannot be
+ * touched. The barrier that waits out an already-running transition is kept
+ * either way; what is given up is only the courtesy resume. Cancelling a pending
+ * resume does not strand the PM core - __pm_runtime_barrier() clears the request
+ * and drains the work - but it does mean the I/O that asked for it is never
+ * served, which is acceptable only because this operation tears MHI down and
+ * rebuilds it anyway. pm_runtime_enable() does not replay it either, so after
+ * the -EBUSY path the device can stay suspended until something asks again.
+ *
+ * One entry state is NOT supported: another owner concurrently disabling
+ * runtime PM. __pm_runtime_disable() returns early when disable_depth is
+ * already nonzero, without running a barrier of its own, and that depth does
+ * not distinguish "already drained" from "someone else is mid-drain, having
+ * dropped power.lock to wait". A PCI-core transition can then still be
+ * executing outside the MHI callback, where MHI_PCI_DEV_SUSPENDED does not see
+ * it. A completed, serialized disable is fine; a concurrent one is not, and
+ * nothing here detects the difference.
+ *
+ * Draining the device's own machinery, in this order, is what makes the quiesce
+ * quiet:
+ *   - MHI_PCI_DEV_QUIESCED, so health_check() stops queueing recovery and
+ *     mhi_pci_recovery_work() returns without touching anything;
+ *   - cancel_work_sync(), then the timer, then cancel_work_sync() again: the
+ *     timer can queue the worker between the first cancellation and its own
+ *     deletion, and that worker would otherwise still be pending when the bit
+ *     is cleared and run a stale recovery against the restored device;
+ *   - mhi_power_down(graceful=false), which forces MHI_PM_LD_ERR_FATAL_DETECT.
+ *     MHI_REG_ACCESS_VALID() excludes it, so the MHI core issues no register
+ *     access from then on.
+ *
+ * None of this can un-start an access already in progress: cancellation waits
+ * for a worker past its own check, and a suspend already inside the endpoint
+ * when this begins has already touched it.
+ *
+ * A device that is already runtime-suspended is rejected rather than handled:
+ * reconciling that power state would mean resuming the endpoint first, over the
+ * link this exists to avoid using. It is re-checked after the barrier, because
+ * a suspend in flight when this started may have completed in the meantime.
+ *
+ * On failure the device is left quiesced with runtime PM still disabled, so the
+ * write can simply be retried; nothing touches the endpoint until one attempt
+ * succeeds.
+ */
+static ssize_t link_recovery_store(struct device *dev,
+				   struct device_attribute *attr,
+				   const char *buf, size_t count)
+{
+	struct pci_dev *pdev = to_pci_dev(dev);
+	struct mhi_pci_device *mhi_pdev = pci_get_drvdata(pdev);
+	struct mhi_controller *mhi_cntrl;
+	struct pci_host_bridge *bridge;
+	unsigned int timeout_ms = 0;
+	u32 id, expect;
+	int err;
+
+	if (!mhi_pdev)
+		return -ENODEV;
+	if (pdev->is_virtfn)
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+
+	/* Parse before anything is touched. kstrtouint() rejects trailing junk. */
+	if (!sysfs_streq(buf, "recover")) {
+		if (strncmp(buf, "recover ", 8))
+			return -EINVAL;
+
+		err = kstrtouint(skip_spaces(buf + 8), 0, &timeout_ms);
+		if (err)
+			return err;
+	}
+
+	mhi_cntrl = &mhi_pdev->mhi_cntrl;
+
+	bridge = pci_find_host_bridge(pdev->bus);
+	if (!bridge || !bridge->link_recover)
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+
+	if (!mhi_pci_sole_device_on_host(pdev, bridge)) {
+		dev_err(&pdev->dev,
+			"refusing link recovery: not the only device on this host\n");
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+	}
+
+	guard(mutex)(&mhi_pdev->recovery_mutex);
+
+	if (test_bit(MHI_PCI_DEV_SUSPENDED, &mhi_pdev->status))
+		return -EBUSY;
+
+	/* Phase 1: stop using the endpoint, and make sure nothing else does. */
+	if (!test_bit(MHI_PCI_DEV_QUIESCED, &mhi_pdev->status)) {
+		__pm_runtime_disable(&pdev->dev, false);
+
+		if (test_bit(MHI_PCI_DEV_SUSPENDED, &mhi_pdev->status)) {
+			pm_runtime_enable(&pdev->dev);
+			return -EBUSY;
+		}
+
+		set_bit(MHI_PCI_DEV_QUIESCED, &mhi_pdev->status);
+
+		cancel_work_sync(&mhi_pdev->recovery_work);
+		timer_delete_sync(&mhi_pdev->health_check_timer);
+		cancel_work_sync(&mhi_pdev->recovery_work);
+
+		if (test_and_clear_bit(MHI_PCI_DEV_STARTED, &mhi_pdev->status)) {
+			mhi_power_down(mhi_cntrl, false);
+			mhi_unprepare_after_power_down(mhi_cntrl);
+		}
+
+		dev_info(&pdev->dev, "quiesced for link recovery\n");
+	}
+
+	/* Phase 2: let the host controller cycle the link. */
+	err = bridge->link_recover(bridge, timeout_ms);
+	if (err) {
+		dev_err(&pdev->dev, "host link recovery failed: %d\n", err);
+		return err;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Phase 3: the host reports the link up and the controller powered, so
+	 * the endpoint can be addressed again. Check it is still the same
+	 * device before writing the saved configuration into it: a modem that
+	 * came back in a different mode would get another device's BARs, and
+	 * every MHI access after that would target the wrong register layout.
+	 * Matching IDs do not prove an unchanged BAR or capability layout, and
+	 * nothing here guarantees the link stays up for the next access.
+	 */
+	expect = (u32)pdev->vendor | ((u32)pdev->device << 16);
+	err = pci_read_config_dword(pdev, PCI_VENDOR_ID, &id);
+	if (err)
+		return pcibios_err_to_errno(err);
+
+	if (id != expect) {
+		dev_err(&pdev->dev,
+			"endpoint came back as %04x:%04x, expected %04x:%04x\n",
+			id & 0xffff, id >> 16, pdev->vendor, pdev->device);
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
+	err = pci_set_power_state(pdev, PCI_D0);
+	if (err)
+		return err;
+
+	err = pci_load_saved_state(pdev, mhi_pdev->pci_state);
+	if (err)
+		return err;
+
+	pci_restore_state(pdev);
+
+	if (!mhi_pci_is_alive(mhi_cntrl)) {
+		dev_err(&pdev->dev, "not alive after link recovery\n");
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
+	err = mhi_prepare_for_power_up(mhi_cntrl);
+	if (err)
+		return err;
+
